      "id": 1,
      "text": "Camptown was founded in 1793 by Captain Job Camp, a Connecticut pioneer who built the first cabin and named the settlement after himself.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/camptown_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town inspired Stephen Foster’s 1850 song “Camptown Races,” linking the community to a classic piece of American music.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camptown%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the early 1800s, lumbering dominated Camptown, with logs floated down Wyalusing Creek to markets in Harrisburg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/camptown_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A permanent covered bridge was erected over Wyalusing Creek in 1855, replacing an earlier bridge that had washed away in 1789.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/camptown_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Camptown sits at the intersection of Pennsylvania Routes 409 and 706, about 4.6 miles north‑northeast of Wyalusing.",
